ody cams and it didn't stop the  brutality that killed him  body cameras are catching on in canada  calgary became the first major city to  adopt them two years ago  with other cities and towns starting to  follow suit  in montreal the mayor is pushing for  them suggesting they would have provided  more evidence  in a recent attack of a police officer  that ended with a black man behind bars  for six  nights before charges against him were  dropped  but in halifax where police issued a  formal apology for interrogating black  people at random  there's a cautious approach and  lingering questions  do cameras help end abuses like racial  profiling  or distract from finding solutions  cameras don't  deal with the underlying issue when it  comes to racial injustice  cameras just capture it doesn't it  doesn't  deal with the issues as halifax  deliberates  toronto is investing 34 million dollars  in the cameras over five years  a big show of faith in technology whose  ultimate value  is unclear ross lord global news  a kansas city chief's assistant coach is  under investigation after being involved  in a serious car crash  brit reid son of head coach andy reid  was involved in a multi-vehicle pileup  in missouri on thursday  three people were injured including two  young children a  five-year-old girl remains in critical  condition police documents show reed  admitted to having  two to three drinks and taking the  stimulant adderall  the 35 year old is not attending the  super bowl  former world heavyweight champion leon  spinks has died  after a five-year bout with cancer  spinks won gold at the 1976 montreal  olympics but his claim to fame came two  years later  when he beat muhammad ali for the  heavyweight title  in just his eighth professional bout  sphinx is the only boxer to take the  title away from ali  seven months later though the two met in  another match and ali  reclaimed the title leon spinks was 67.  still ahead why a new batman is breaking  barriers  for comic book diversity  you're watching global national  dc comics has debuted a new batman for  the first time in 82 years a person of  color  is putting on the cal of the cape  crusader and the writer behind this  superhero  is also black some readers see this as a  step in the right direction for ethnic  representation  as phil carpenter reports the industry  still has a long way to go  to improve diversity in comic books  at first illustrator latif martin was  skeptical when he heard about dc comics  new superhero character he thought it'd  be just another story paying lip service  to diversity  you know black is in let's cash in on it  in december the comic book giant  revealed that the next  version of batman would have a black man  behind the cowl  he appears in a new four-part comic  series called  future state the new batman once he got  a copy  martin became less cynical partly  because the character portraying batman  is so well known to long time fans  and wasn't just suddenly invented to  fill a quarter tim  fox there's a relationship with batman  with the whole lineage  of uh you know lucius fox being the  person who you know created all the gear  for him  martin is happy because the writer  behind the work  is also a man of color john ridley the  oscar-winning screenwriter of 12 years a  slave  ridley was thrilled to help evolve a  character for his kids  and other kids of color who love these  stories but just  don't quite see themselves in these  stories  that means the most to me martin agrees  that it helps for kids to see themselves  in these stories  as a black kid in montreal he rarely saw  someone  he could really relate to in comic books  and on tv  time and again i'm always disappointed  oh where's the black guy oh he's the  butler okay  where's the black guy oh he's a pimp  where's the black oh he's the thug  tara paul co-hosts a podcast about comic  books  she and her colleagues have been pushing  for more diversity in comics too  we've been waiting for more  representation that  folks that can represent us and the  superhero world  with this character though ridley wants  readers to go beyond  skin color he says the story is less  about the character's race  than his experiences as a person this is  not black batman  this is jace fox who is a person of  color who is black  who becomes batman there are those who  say that with the push for diversity in  representation comes the risk of  tokenism which some experts feel media  giants like dc and marvel  struggle with the name black often  appears in their name  their superhero name as if to say that  you know that's the defining feature  right  some have argued that even the name  black panther is an example of tokenism  i really think it's all about how the  thing is treated  and also who is behind the scenes  really agrees that diversity among the  creators is also necessary  to help make the characters more  realistic though he believes there is  room for improvement  he sees changes for the better there is  a desire  to have characters that are more  reflective  of the world that we live in making the  colorful world of comics a place  for all kinds of new characters phil  carpenter global news  montreal that story is part of a new  initiative on global called perspectives  the site features stories meant to cover  and reflect canada's diverse populations  check it out at globalnews.ca  perspectives up next canadian star dan  levy plays host and gives us more to  laugh about  somewhere my 13 year old self has  fainted in like a really needy  melodramatic way  hilarious that is canadian actor dan  levy gushing about hosting saturday  night live last night  the shit's creek star joked about  covid's safety precautions and took the  audience on a tour of the new york  studio  where he ran into another beloved  canadian  [Applause]  now i flew in i flew in to wish you luck  tonight  but because i traveled i'm now in this  high  isolation box  father and son have a lot to celebrate  these days shit's creek swept  every comedy category at the emmys it's  now in line to do the same thing at the  golden globes  and the screen actors guild awards  that is awesome and that is global  national for this sunday i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is prince albert  saskatchewan  we would love to see your part of this  country so email your photos to viewers  at globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna friesen  will be at the anchor desk tomorrow  have a good night and i'll see you next  weekend  [Music]  you
 

